{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790448416,"nonce":"DHNNlSOJAfpq4fyG5fnMw8CQUdM=","title":"Once a static method has run, instances of its class answer it too","message":"Interpreter and tiered:\n\n```wren\nclass C {\n  construct new() {}\n  static make() { \"static make\" }\n}\nSystem.print(C.make())\nvar f = Fiber.new { C.new().make() }\nSystem.print(f.try())  // static make\nSystem.print(f.error)  // null, expected C does not implement 'make()'\n```\n\nWithout the first `C.make()` the instance call raises as it should, so\nsomething caches the static lookup under the class pointer and an\ninstance whose class is that pointer hits it. Static calls are cached\nunder the class's own pointer (identity guard) and instance calls under\nthe receiver's class, and both are the same pointer here.\n\nAOT direct calls check identity for statics, so AOT builds raise here\nwhile the runtime runs the static body.","files":null}]}